# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MOASYS-Vault — Movies ignore list (EXAMPLE)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A "do not warn me about this" list for your movies library. Anything listed
# here is silently dropped from output/<drive>/movies/warnings.json and
# validation-warnings.json on every run, and counted in the run summary.
#
# Ignore lists are per DRIVE as well as per type, because warning paths are
# relative to that drive's root_path. `ignored/server/movies.yaml` only
# affects runs against the root named "Server".
#
# How to use this file:
#   1. Copy it to `ignored/<drive>/movies.yaml` (drop the `.example` suffix).
#      The real file is gitignored so your personal decisions stay out of
#      version control.
#   2. Add names under the level they belong to.
#   3. Re-run `npm run movies <drive>`. Matching warnings disappear.
#
# ── Levels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   folders   a category folder      - Other HD
#   movies    one movie folder       - The Crow (1994)
#   files     one file, qualified    - The Crow (1994)/The Crow (1994).mkv
#
# Names are matched at their own level and are CATEGORY-INDEPENDENT: a movie
# listed here is silenced wherever it lives — HD/, Other HD/, or both. That is
# also what lets an entry reach warn_multi_quality and warn_duplicate_quality,
# which report a bare title with no category to anchor to.
#
# ── Qualifiers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `files:` entries must name their movie folder too — a bare filename would
# match that name anywhere in the library. A `/` means "somewhere above", not
# "immediately above", so you can skip intermediate levels.
#
# ── Scope ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An entry silences EVERY warning at or below its level — naming, TMDB,
# quality, all of them. There is no per-warning-type scoping. To turn off a
# whole warning type instead, set `checks.warn_*: false` in rules/movies.yaml.
#
# ── Matching ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Exact per level (not a prefix, so `The Crow (1994)` does not match
# `The Crow 2 (1996)`), case-insensitive, and either slash works — the same
# file is correct on Windows and macOS/Linux.
#
# ── Finding names ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Take them from the `path` values in output/<drive>/movies/warnings.json and
# validation-warnings.json: the last segment for a movie, the last two for a
# file. Drop the leading category folder.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# Silence everything under a category folder
# (Useful for "Other UHD" / "Other HD" / "Other SD" — categories you keep
# around but don't actively curate.)
# folders:
#   - Other UHD
#   - Other SD


# Silence one movie, in every category it appears in
# (Drops every warning on it — quality mismatch, TMDB no-match, naming, and
# the duplicate/multi-quality checks that no path-based entry could reach.)
# movies:
#   - Some Movie (2020)
#   - Luxo Jr. (1986)
#
# `{edition-...}` is part of the folder name, so each edition is its own
# folder and needs its own line. `The Hobbit (2014)` does NOT cover
# `The Hobbit (2014) {edition-Maple Films Edit}`.
#   - The Hobbit (2014)
#   - The Hobbit (2014) {edition-Maple Films Edit}


# Silence one file
# (Pin-point. Useful for a single version with a runtime or quality outlier
# you've decided to accept. Note this still silences that file's OTHER
# warnings — if you only want to drop one check across the library, use
# `checks.warn_*: false` in rules/movies.yaml instead.)
# files:
#   - Blade Runner (1982)/Blade Runner (1982) {edition-Final Cut}.mp4
